A||N||4||jhogans||Building Show Cars????||||1||
Z||000000||jhogans||06-03-2000||10:30 PM||||How do you build a show car?  What does it take to build one?  I've been thinking of turning my Fiero into a show car and compete at various cars shows.  How do I go about doing this?  What does it take to win?  Thanks for any info.<P>ie.  I know you need money  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||sdm1964||06-04-2000||06:12 AM||||It depends on if you are going stock, street or custom. I was in daytona in march and here is what i found out. Stock, not too many as a matter of fact their were no stock 85 gt's. street, 2 85 gt's. One pace car. custom, tons of cars. I was in the steet 85 gt class. It was my first show in a car owned for 5 months. Clean it good and pay attention to the small details. Mine had some over spray from former owner. Lost first by 1/2 a point. It will not happen again next year.If you want to win go for the classes no one is in, mainly stock or pace car. Make it a new looking as possible, interior, paint etc. I want to show mine, but i bought it to drive. From what i saw if you go for custom you need to have tons of money, there were some heavly modified ones that were sharp, way too much money for my wallet. If you have the money order this photo cd from the daytona show, <A HREF="http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/daytona2000/." TARGET=_blank>http://members.yourlink.net/dragon/daytona2000/.</A>  it should give you a good idea what you will be up against.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jhogans||06-07-2000||12:28 AM||||Thanks for the info.  Anybody else on the board compete||||reg||1
Z||000003||sdm1964||06-07-2000||05:56 AM||||There were at least 3 other people in daytona this year from the forum. old lars, Phill, but  right now he is on the power tour, and one other. ||||reg||14
Z||000004||Old Lar||06-10-2000||12:02 AM||||I take my Fiero to lots of shows.  Depending on the judging, you win some and you lose some. I just got back from the Dells Run 2000 (last week).  I went on a road trip to Kansas City then stopped in Paxton Ill to see Bob Steiger.  I put on 675 miles today coming back to upstate NY from Ill.  My car turned 80000 miles today.<P>I go to the shows to meet people and to see what they are doing to their cars.  Once I get all my photos together, I'll stick them in a Zing Album.  I have 5 rolls to get processed.<P>As far as competing, my 87 is stock.  Keeping it clean and driving it several hundred miles to shows is a lot of clean up work.  I drove in the rain to Wisconsin.  The bugs don't stick to the front, but road spray is everywhere.  Stock may be easier to compete because there are fewer stock cars going to the shows.  The custom class or modified classes are real hard.  There are a lot of cars out there and a lot of real nice cars.  Depending on the judging, peer judging or popular choice can be fickle.  At Daytona they had a panel of judges evaluating the cars.  That way you are all on the same level, judged against the same criteria.<P>The real bite is when you end up next to a 3000 mile trailer queen, and you've driven your Fiero 800 mile to a show.  Of course the trailer queen beats you out every time.<P>White cars don't win trophys, unless you have Jim Hallman's 88 GT w t-tops.  Best of manual fast backs at the Dells.||||reg||1
